{"availability":"available","full":"onSec-fr/KrbNixPwn","name":"KrbNixPwn","description":"A Native Bash Framework for Kerberos Ticket Extraction on Linux 🔐","category":"Developer tools","language":"Shell","stars":48,"forks":3,"created":"2025-11-24T16:08:35Z","updated":"2026-02-23T15:08:31Z","branch":"main","homepage":"","topics":[],"demo":"https://orange-cyberdefense.github.io/GOAD/extensions/lx01/","color":"#302e55","ink":"#dbd2ff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-09T16:43:40.584Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-09T16:43:40.584Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-09T16:43:40.584Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-09T16:43:40.584Z","stars":48,"forks":3}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Linux systems are increasingly required to participate in enterprise identity management infrastructures alongside Windows environments, making Kerberos-based authentication ubiquitous in hybrid environments. However, while Windows red-team tooling (e.g., Rubeus) is mature, Linux Kerberos tooling remains fragmented :","KrbNixPwn was developed to address these gaps:"],"features":["Existing tools are task-specific, each supporting only one cache mechanism or limited extraction logic.","Many rely on Python or heavy external libraries, which is not always acceptable in restrictive red-team contexts.","Most operate in a one-shot fashion, unsuitable for long engagements where new user sessions appear over time.","Unified toolchain supporting all major cache backends.","Zero-to-minimal dependencies through pure Bash + coreutils."],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/onSec-fr/KrbNixPwn/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"a3083e9bf9b0d4ea9d70fe4a55803a9b73d4f70aedf55074c8c08dc6ba0f79c7","extractedAt":"2026-10-09T16:43:40.699Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-09T16:43:40.699Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"Linux systems are increasingly required to participate in enterprise identity management infrastructures alongside Windows environments, making Kerberos-based au